Sure, let’s drop the map for a moment and just use two nested loops—the most obvious way.  

```javascript
function twoSum(nums, target) {
  for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
    for (let j = i + 1; j < nums.length; j++) {
      if (nums[i] + nums[j] === target) return [i, j];
    }
  }
}
```  

Here we literally try every pair until we find the right sum. It works, but notice we’re checking many pairs over and over.  

Now, think about what the map version is doing instead of that inner loop. What single piece of information does it store for each i that lets us skip the whole inner loop?
